A||||8||PAUL88GT||Factory stereo questions||||5||
Z||000000||PAUL88GT||12-15-1999||01:01 PM||||The "Performance Sound" slider in my 88gt seems to have no effect on the sound of the stereo system.  Anyone have any ideas on trouble-shooting this problem?  Also, is the the "sub-woofer" I sometimes hear discussed?  I have only had the car a few months.<P>Merry Christmas from North Alabama!||||reg||5
Z||000001||DRH||12-15-1999||01:41 PM||||The slider only controls the volume of the sub-woofer. You should notice more bass sound when you slide it to the right. <P>For trouble shooting: <BR>Did it ever work? <BR>Is the radio factory or aftermarket? (there is a trick to hooking up an aftermarket radio to get the sub to work) <BR>Is the sub still there or did a previous owner remove it? (the speaker is mounted in a plastic box tucked under the dash on the passenger side)<BR>Next steps would be to check speaker (could be blown), wiring, amp, etc... I can look in the service manual for procedure once you eliminate the obvious.<P>David.||||reg||1
Z||000002||dogtired||12-15-1999||02:59 PM||||DHR covered most of the bases. You might check inside the sub-box to see if there is even a speaker in there.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I have seen three, different cars that had that speaker taken out because it was bad. Some folks just un hook the wire on the left side of the sub-box, to keep it from ratteling.  Good Luck||||reg||1
Z||000003||PAUL88GT||12-15-1999||05:24 PM||||Thanks. I did see an unattached wire under the passenger side dash.  Previous owner may have unplugged it.  Assuming the speaker is bad, can you recommend a replacement speaker and supplier?<BR>Paul||||reg||7
Z||000004||DRH||12-15-1999||06:26 PM||||I currently have a regular 5.25 speaker rigged up in mine. Works OK but I can't turn it very loud.<P>The factory sub is an unusual size (5") and the frame is designed to mate with the mounting bracket. Pontiac dealer says they are discontinued.<P>I got a lead from someone that you could get them from Speaker City in Los Angeles (818-846-9921) for $38 but have not checked it out yet.<P>If the speaker is still there and just has a bad cone, re-coning might be a possiblity if you can find someone in your area.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||JetroGT||12-16-1999||05:27 AM||||I've got two of the subs. And both are messed up. I found a place that would fix them but never got around to it.<BR>Hear is the response they gave me.<P>FYI<P>Your woofers can be repaired and tweaked in our lab<BR>for $32. ea. plus shipping.<P>That is for re-edging.  After seeing them and really finding out the<BR>problem<BR>with the second one you mentioned, I will call you with the options.<P><BR>Naturally removing the drivers from the cabinets is the<BR>preferred method for shipping. Please include your name<BR>and address and daytime phone number when you ship.<P>Payment arrangements can be made by phone once your repair<BR>is completed.  Visa, MC & Amex accepted.<P>Packing suggestion:  Bolt both woofers face to face and<BR>wrap them in a plastic bag followed by newspaper.  This<BR>keeps packing peanuts out of the woofers.  Then place<BR>them into a box full of packing peanuts or suitable<BR>material.<P>I oversee all of the repairs myself, so they're in good<BR>hands (ears).<P> -Steve Deckert<BR>  High Fidelity Engineering Co.<BR>  103 Spring Street<BR>  Peoria IL 61603<BR>  (309) 671-2428<BR>  Site: <A HREF="http://www.decware.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.decware.com</A> <BR>  Repairs: <A HREF="http://www.decware.com/repairs.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.decware.com/repairs.htm</A> <BR>  Parts: <A HREF="http://www.decware.com/parts.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.decware.com/parts.htm</A> <BR>  How to fix your own speakers: <A HREF="http://www.decware.com/surround.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.decware.com/surround.htm</A> ||||reg||3
Z||000006||PAUL88GT||12-16-1999||01:11 PM||||I plugged in the sub-woofer.  It worked, but was intermittant (went on and off every second or so). Very annoying, I understand why it was unplugged.  I sounded like it was a bad connection somewhere or it was shorting out.  On a previous thread TOM noted the following replacement was available:<P>Speaker City 818 846 9921<BR>Versa-Tronics D0B130R-PP/8<BR>$38 I think<P>I prefer to go with a new one than rebuild a 12 year old one. Thanks again<p>[This message has been edited by PAUL88GT (edited 12-16-1999).]||||reg||3
Z||000007||william_thorniii||12-16-1999||07:27 PM||||Paul,<P>If the problem is intermittant, then it is definitely not your speaker.  It sounds like you need to tear into your dash and find where you are losing your (probably ground) short.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000008||ChadW13||12-16-1999||09:50 PM||||I have an 85'SE w/sub.  A few years ago I ran into the same problem and went to my local audio shop who informed me about the non standard 5" sub.  They fabricated an adaptor which extended the location of the sub 2" downward.  This enabled me to use a 6" Kicker free air sub ( free air b/c of lack of air space) which cost about $70.00.  Not only did I get better base, but also a replacement standard.  The audio shop did not charge me for the adaptor but if they did, it would have been worth it. ||||reg||1
